# Golden fixtures are byte-exact LF sources. The Rust backend embeds
# tests/golden/basics/main.rs via include_str! and slices its preamble at "\n"
# anchors, and every golden test compares emitted bytes against these files. A
# Windows checkout with autocrlf must never rewrite them to CRLF, or the anchor
# splice fails (IPE-I0203: golden anchor not found) and byte compares diverge.
tests/golden/** text eol=lf

# The Rust backend embeds its emit templates via include_str! and splices them
# at "\n" anchors (e.g. "// USER TYPES\n" in main.rs). A Windows autocrlf
# checkout that rewrote them to CRLF makes the anchor search fail (IPE-I0203:
# golden anchor not found), so pin the whole templates tree to LF.
src/compiler/backend/rust/templates/** text eol=lf

# Binary artifact classes. Marking these `binary` (= `-text -diff`) stops git
# from attempting line-ending normalization, text diffs, or three-way merges on
# non-text content — which would corrupt the bytes and produce useless diffs.
# Build output (*.rlib/*.rmeta/*.a/*.o/*.so/*.dylib/*.wasm) should never be
# committed at all (see .gitignore + the artifact-guard CI job), but if one
# slips in, git must treat it as opaque bytes. Images and archives are legit
# tracked fixtures (example snapshots, vendored assets) and are likewise binary.
*.rlib   binary
*.rmeta  binary
*.a      binary
*.o      binary
*.so     binary
*.dylib  binary
*.dll    binary
*.exe    binary
*.wasm   binary
*.png    binary
*.jpg    binary
*.jpeg   binary
*.gif    binary
*.ico    binary
*.webp   binary
*.pdf    binary
*.zip    binary
*.gz     binary
*.tar    binary
*.bz2    binary
*.xz     binary
*.zst    binary
